module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.modifier_accepted_on_a_shape_its_check_cannot_see

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.rung_climb { AwaitsOneGrounding, GroundingUndeclared }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, StructurallyGuaranteed }

data modifier_accepted_on_a_shape_its_check_cannot_see: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "modifier_accepted_on_a_shape_its_check_cannot_see" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: MechanicallyPreventable }, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: AwaitsOneGrounding { grounding: GroundingUndeclared }, trigger_capability: "emission targets that read the sole_constructor property off a coproduct declaration and refuse the foreign variant authoring in the emitted program, so the guarantee holds on source->each emission target and not only source->accepted .dag" } },

  receipts: [
    "**modifier accepted on a shape its check cannot see** (a declaration-site modifier that names a guarantee parses on a shape the enforcing pass never inspects, so the modifier is present, the source reads as walled, and nothing enforces it.",

    "**THIS ROW IS NOT THE AUTHORITY ON ITS OWN SPECIMEN, AND THE FIRST REVISION CLAIMED TO BE, which is the most useful thing in it.** The `sole_constructor`-on-a-coproduct hole is ALREADY DECLARED, at `v1.compiler.parse` above `alias_rhs_is_anonymous_record`, as a §4b(2) class that CAN CLIMB AFTER ONE GROUNDING, with a named next-rung trigger; it is measured by `test.claim.sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe` `f13_variant_construction_refuses`, and `test.fixture.sole_constructor_variant_probe.definer` declares `type SealedChoice sole_constructor = SealedA { .. } | SealedB { .. }` FOR THE PURPOSE of measuring it. What belongs here is only the general recognition rule; the specimen's population, ceiling and trigger belong to that declaration and are cited rather than restated.",

    "**THREE CLAIMS FROM THE FIRST REVISION ARE WITHDRAWN, all in the same direction — treating a rediscovery as a discovery.** (1) `WHY IT SURVIVED: every prior use was a record` is FALSE: a coproduct use exists and is deliberate. (2) `HOW IT WAS FOUND` narrated a lane discovering the gap; the lane rediscovered a documented one, and the documentation was one grep from the code it was reading. (3) The proposed remedy — refuse the modifier at parse time on a sum — CONTRADICTS THE STANDING RULING, which rejected exactly that with a reason the first revision never engaged: refusing there breaks the fixture that exists to measure the hole, and refusing only the harmless leaf-alias arm would refuse where nothing is authored and stay silent in the arm that matters. The declared climb is to wire coproduct construction through `sole_constructor_construction_diags` FIRST, after which the whole `=` family is threaded or refused in one motion by the lane that owns the wall.",

    "**THE COST OF THE FIRST REVISION IS THE POINT.** A row was filed asserting novelty, a fix was recommended to an operator on that basis, and the operator APPROVED IT. Had the approval been executed, the change would have broken an enrolled probe in order to close a hole that probe exists to report, and the ledger would have carried a second authority contradicting a live declaration — the §3 violation this ledger exists to prevent, committed by the mechanism meant to prevent it. It was caught by reading the parse site before editing it, which is one step later than it should have been caught.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE, AND IT IS TWO RULES.** First, the class: a declaration-site modifier is not evidence that a check ran. Write the state the modifier forbids, in the position a real violator would write it, and watch it be refused; a modifier that parses is a parser result, a refusal is an enforcement result, and §4b(1) counts only the second. This generalizes past `sole_constructor` to every modifier, attribute or annotation whose enforcement lives in a pass keyed on something the declaration does not itself carry.",

    "Second, and it is the one this row was written to earn: **BEFORE FILING A GAP, GREP THE SITE THAT WOULD IMPLEMENT THE FIX.** A hole deliberately left open is usually documented AT the code that would close it, not in the ledger where a search for the class name would find it — so the ledger is the wrong instrument for the question `is this already known`, and the right one is reading the function you are about to edit. An empirical probe proves the hole EXISTS; it says nothing about whether the hole is NEWS, and the two were conflated here.",

    "**THE NEIGHBOUR IS `prose_asserts_an_enforcement_no_operation_can_produce` and the boundary is the carrier:** there the false claim is in prose a machine cannot read, here it is in SYNTAX THE COMPILER ACCEPTED, which is strictly more convincing and therefore strictly more dangerous. Against `discriminating_arm_built_but_never_enrolled`: there a real check exists and nothing calls it; here the check is called, runs, and answers the wrong question because its key does not match the shape.",

    "**RUNG AND CEILING FOR THE CLASS AS A CLASS**, which is all this row may speak to: the general mismatch between a modifier's accepted shapes and its enforcing pass's covered shapes is decidable per modifier and undecidable as a blanket property, so it stays a per-modifier obligation discharged where the modifier is minted. The specimen's own rung, ceiling and trigger are the parse site's to state and are not restated here.)",
    "**A SECOND SPECIMEN OF THE ALREADY-DECLARED CLASS, MEASURED THREE WAYS IN gunbc#14292 (swift-wren-451, 2026-10-18). IT CLAIMS NO NOVELTY AND ADDS NO NEW GAP** — it is the same modifier-on-an-uninspected-shape hole seen from the other side, recorded because the three-source measurement is what tells a lane which guarantee it actually holds. The specimen: `data FirmwareNetworkBootStanding sole_constructor = Ready { evidence: ReadyEvidence } | NeedsPreparation { ... }`, the modifier on the SUM with its payloads as anonymous arms, and three probe sources compiled through `compile_dag_diagnostic_census`. (1) A FOREIGN module writing the sealed payload record's literal yields `SoleConstructorViolation` >= 1 and the census runs. (2) That IDENTICAL literal wrapped in a hand-written arm of the sealed sum yields the SAME count — a delta of ZERO — so the sum-level modifier refuses nothing. (3) A lawful foreign module importing the same names and constructing no verdict yields 0, which is what makes (1) a refusal rather than a compile failure. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE PAIR (1)+(2), NOT (1) ALONE: a probe that merely claims `(1) >= 1` is a FALSE GREEN here, because the payload seal is enforced on its own and stays red whether or not the sum modifier does anything — which is exactly what an earlier revision of this probe did, and it went unremarked until the delta was measured. CONSEQUENCE FOR A LANE, and the practical rule the fleet should rely on: never read a union-level `sole_constructor` as a wall. Its enforcement is keyed on the payload's identity, which is precisely the thing a sum does not carry, so any arm of any sealed sum is constructible by hand. The walls that ARE enforced, and that a model should rest on, are (a) the modifier on the PAYLOAD RECORD, which the compiler refuses at `alias_rhs_is_anonymous_record` (source 1 above), and (b) `admit_callers` on the minting function, enforced as `ConstructorCallAdmissionRefused` naming the function, so a call that skips the gate does not resolve. A model honest about which of those two it holds is honest; one that reads the sum modifier as the wall is in the state this row exists to name. Population, ceiling and the next-rung trigger for the specimen stay the parse site's to state above `alias_rhs_is_anonymous_record` at `v1.compiler.parse`, measured by `test.claim.sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe` `f13_variant_construction_refuses` — cited, not restated. What this specimen contributes to the RUNG is narrow and is the only new thing: the compiler-side climb (wiring coproduct construction through `sole_constructor_construction_diags`) can be MEASURED from the fleet layer today, with `blocking_subject_count_for` over three sources, so a lane need not wait for that compiler change to find out which of its seals are real.",
    "**COPRODUCT ARM CLOSED IN THE SEED (stern-bear-100, gunbc#15451), ENUMERATION OF SHAPES THE MODIFIER IS ACCEPTED ON.** `type X sole_constructor ...` is accepted on: (a) the record body `{..}` and (b) the `= { .. }` record spelling, both enforced; (c) the leaf alias `= Other`, carried on the declaration by type_item_from_alias_rhs and judged when X is constructed; (d) the coproduct `= A {..} | B {..}` in both the leading-pipe and bare spellings. CORRECTION TO THE BRIEF THAT SPAWNED THIS CLOSE: the seal was dropped at PARSE on (d) (parse_type_body_after_eq built both coproduct Nodes with properties: []), not only at infer; both constructors now carry it, and sole_constructor_construction_diags resolves the constructed name through variant_owner_node, so one keyed SoleConstructorViolation is emitted per foreign variant literal and a foreign match draws none. EVIDENCE (RED + CONTROL, enrolled as permanent regression controls, §4b(4)): test.claim.sole_constructor_coproduct_witness (exactly one violation keyed SealedA; foreign match of every variant draws none) and test.claim.sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe f13_variant_construction_refuses, which is no longer expected-red. Measured on a local release seed old vs new: forge 0 -> 1; 68 importer and std.durable_compare_and_set closures 0 violations and 0 blocking before and after. RUNG: source->accepted .dag climbed from below the ladder (silently accepted) to MechanicallyPreventable (the check is a compiler pass keyed on the owner; the minimum across paths is held there because emission targets never read the property and the v2 native wall was not executed from this lane). Still accepted-but-unchecked: none at parse; the emission-target gap above is the next-rung trigger. Population sealed: gunbc.model.population PopulationProvenance, gunbc.host.managed_host ManagedHostMembership, test.fixture.sole_constructor_variant_probe SealedChoice. CasOutcome and the FileHold*Outcome family are follow-ups (CasOutcome needs gunbc#15433 and gunbc.auth.gcp_iam_bootstrap_journal, which constructs CasCommitted outside its module).",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
